text="Summary">Summary</fr:title><fr:date><fr:year>2024</fr:year><fr:month>6</fr:month><fr:day>3</fr:day></fr:date><fr:date><fr:year>2024</fr:year><fr:month>8</fr:month><fr:day>21</fr:day></fr:date><fr:authors /></fr:frontmatter><fr:mainmatter><fr:p>Ignoring the natural numbers type and the idiosyncratic list type of
        <fr:link
type="local"
href="johnstone-2002.xml"
addr="johnstone-2002"
title="Sketches of an Elephant: a topos theory compendium">Sketches of an Elephant: a topos theory compendium</fr:link>, there is a core concept (using the notion of
        elementary topops for reference) of <fr:link
type="local"
href="hejohns-000F.xml"
addr="hejohns-000F"
title="&quot;Higher-order intuitionistic logic&quot; v. &quot;topos logic&quot;">"finite-type intuitionistic
        predicate logic" aka "topos logic" aka "higher-order intuitionistic
        logic"</fr:link>, although certain inter-definable choices can be
        made.</fr:p><fr:p>There's no clear notion of "taking everything", but a reasonable
        "logical" choice seems to be that (over a language <fr:tex
display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathcal {L}]]></fr:tex>,) the types are
        <fr:tex
display="inline"><![CDATA[→,×,1]]></fr:tex>, the terms are <fr:tex
display="inline"><![CDATA[x,λ,f t,⟨t,u⟩,πᵢ,\{x|ϕ(x)\},*]]></fr:tex>, and the
        formulas are <fr:tex
display="inline"><![CDATA[=,R(\overline {x}),∈,∧,∨,¬,⟹,∀,∃]]></fr:tex>.</fr:p><fr:p>One curiosity with taking a type <fr:tex
display="inline"><![CDATA[Ω]]></fr:tex> and formulas to <fr:em>just</fr:em> be
        terms of type <fr:tex
display="inline"><![CDATA[Ω]]></fr:tex>, is that we then have formulas like <fr:tex
display="inline"><![CDATA[π₁ ⟨⊤,⊥⟩]]></fr:tex>
        (definitionally equal to <fr:tex
display="inline"><![CDATA[⊤]]></fr:tex>) which don't seem very formula-like.</fr:p></fr:mainmatter><fr:backmatter /></fr:tree></fr:mainmatter><fr:backmatter /></fr:tree></fr:mainmatter><fr:backmatter /></fr:tree></fr:backmatter></fr:tree>